== Backward Compatibility ==

Older wallets and nodes that have not been made compatible with SegWit version 3 and P2SG will not understand these outputs. Per [[bip-0350.mediawiki|BIP 350]] older wallets are able to spend funds to SegWit version 3 outputs in a PSBT transaction, but an upgraded wallet will be needed to complete the transaction.

Users should ensure they are using updated wallets and nodes to receive P2SG outputs and validate transactions using P2SG outputs. P2SG is fully compatible with tapscript and existing tapscript programs can be used in P2SG outputs without modification. P2SG can also support future scripts with new leaf versions.

== Applications ==

=== Lightning Symmetry ===

==== Eliminating the 2x-Delay ====

Lightning Symmetry (LN-Symmetry) suffers from the [https://bitcoinops.org/en/newsletters/2025/01/10/#contract-level-relative-timelocks 2x-delay problem], caused by the relative timelock of the settlement transaction being reset each time an update transaction is confirmed on-chain. This doubles the timelock in HTLCs, reducing the available liquidity and the attractiveness of LN-Symmetry over existing alternatives.

P2SG outputs solve this problem by enabling a contract-level relative timelock at settlement using Greg Sanders' [https://delvingbitcoin.org/t/contract-level-relative-timelocks-or-lets-talk-about-ancestry-proofs-and-singletons/1353 proposed] <code>funding->kickoff->update->settle</code> architecture. With P2SG, this can be achieved solely using opcodes already in consideration for LN-Symmetry (such as <code>OP_CSFS</code> [[bip-0348.md|BIP 348]] and <code>OP_TEMPLATEHASH</code> [[bip-0446.md|BIP 446]]).

The primary change over standard LN-Symmetry is the inclusion of a kickoff transaction, which includes two outputs, a P2SG output to hold the channel funds for the update path and a P2TR (or P2MR) dust output that acts as a contract-level relative timelock and cannot be spent except alongside a singleton with an identifier matching the first. The settlement transaction concludes the channel by consuming the singleton and the timelock simultaneously, thereby enforcing a contract-level relative timelock.

<pre>
  [ FUNDING TX ]
         │
         ▼
  [ KICKOFF TX ]
  ├── OUTPUT 0: P2SG (Update Path Funds)
  └── OUTPUT 1: P2TR (Timelock)
         │               │
         ▼               │
  [ UPDATE TX 1 ]        │
         │               │
         ▼               │
  [ UPDATE TX N ]        │ (Consumes Timelock)
         │               ▼
         │        [ SETTLEMENT TX ]
         └──────▶ (Input 0: Update N Output 0)
                  (Input 1: Kickoff Output 1)
</pre>

Sibling commitments can be emulated by committing to zero-value anyone-can-spend outputs that enforce either genesis or continuation. For instance, the settlement transaction can include a zero-value anyone-can-spend P2SG output that continues the singleton created by the kickoff transaction and another that commits via genesis to an input spending the timelock. Each input can then commit to the presence of the other using <code>OP_TEMPLATEHASH</code> or <code>SIGHASH_ALL|ANYONECANPAY</code>, respectively.

Though out-of-scope for this BIP, we can eliminate these P2SG outputs with two additional capabilities:
1. The ability to push the identifier of a spent singleton at a specific index.
2. The ability to push a modified <code>OP_TEMPLATEHASH</code> that commits to the prevout at the next index, if present.

With the former, the timelock output approves any transaction that consumes the singleton at input 0, provided the relative timelock is satisfied. With the latter, the settlement hash commits directly to the timelock being consumed at input 1, and update transactions are precluded from spending the timelock prematurely.

(1) can be safely used without (2), but the protocol must reverse the order of the two inputs in the settlement transaction.

=== ZK-Rollups ===

P2SG also provides a foundation for future zero-knowledge rollups, like [https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/068 ShieldedCSV], to support non-interactive bridging.

By creating an identifier that is unique to the L2 and independent of state, P2SG enables a future upgrade that allows users to condition spends on the presence of the L2 in the transaction. This would require a capability that is out-of-scope for this BIP, but for the purpose of illustrating the architectural benefits of P2SG, we will assume this capability exists. The key idea is that by delegating their UTXO to the singleton, a user can safely make a deposit directly to the L2 without state contention or interactivity.

To the extent necessary, users would also commit to deposit conditions, such as the amount and receive address, in a manner the singleton is forced to respect. This might be via the annex, an output commitment, an adaptor signature, or an alternative.

After signing, users would broadcast their deposits over a dedicated public network, and transaction builders would aggregate pending deposits and compete to produce transactions with valid proofs, capturing a portion of the total fee. Completed transactions would then be broadcast and added to the public mempool. Alternatively, users might scan their local mempool for transactions interacting with the L2 and append their deposit directly via RBF, generating the new proof locally.

== Related Work ==

The singleton concept has been discussed a number of times in the Bitcoin community. For instance, [https://delvingbitcoin.org/t/contract-level-relative-timelocks-or-lets-talk-about-ancestry-proofs-and-singletons/1353 this Delving thread] discusses the use of ancestor proofs to enforce contract-level relative timelocks in Lightning Symmetry. Participants discuss the challenges of implementing this capability, suggesting the use of opcodes like <code>OP_CAT</code> [[bip-0347.mediawiki|BIP 347]] to implement recursive covenants that validate the parent and grandparent transactions, an idea previously discussed [https://gnusha.org/pi/bitcoindev/20220310064717.GA7597@erisian.com.au/ on the mailing list].

While recursive introspection is a viable way to enforce singleton behavior, it has several drawbacks over P2SG's approach. For one, it is considerably more expensive to implement, requiring a large witness, complex script, and new introspection opcodes. The approach also imposes an asymmetric cost on users, where the amortized cost of interacting with the singleton could rise considerably if the parent or grandparent transactions have many more inputs or outputs than usual. The only conceivable way to eliminate this asymmetry is with a zero-knowledge proof, which adds considerable cost and complexity. Finally, a script-based approach makes it more challenging to implement standardized introspection of the singleton identifier.

[https://github.com/JohnLaw2/btc-iids/blob/main/iids14.pdf Inherited IDs] is another proposal that offers ancestor-proof capabilities. Unlike P2SG, however, the Inherited ID proposal requires nodes to store additional state outside the UTXO set and lacks support for stable identifiers.

